r the normal cases. Let me look for integer overflow issues.\n\nIn `name_length()`:\n```c\nn += (*encoded == \'.\' || *encoded == \'\\\\\') ? 2 : 1;\n```\n\n`n` is an `int`. If the name is very long (many labels), `n` could overflow. But `n` is used to allocate memory in `ares_expand_name`: `*s = ares_malloc(nlen.uns + 1)`. If `n` overflows to a small value, the allocation would be too small, and `ares_expand_name` would write past the end of the buffer.\n\nBut wait, `n` counts the expanded length of the name, including escape sequences. Each label byte contributes at most 2 to `n`. The maximum DNS name is 255 bytes (wire format), so the expanded name is at most 510 bytes (if every byte is \'.\' or \'\\\\\'). Plus separators. So `n` can be at most about 510 + 127 = 637. No overflow.\n\nHmm, but what about the case where there are many indirections? Each indirection follows a pointer to another location, and the labels at that location are counted. But the total number of label bytes is still bounded by the buffer size.\n\nWait, actually, `name_length` doesn\'t track which labels it has already counted. If there are multiple indirections that point to the same location, the labels at that location would be counted multiple times! This could cause `n` to overflow!\n\nFor example:\n1. Start at offset 12: `0xc0 0x14` (indirection to offset 20)\n2. At offset 20: `0xc0 0x14` (indirection to offset 20 - self-reference!)\n3. `indir` counter increments: 1, 2, 3, ... up to MAX_INDIRS (50)\n4. Each time, the labels at offset 20 are counted\n\nBut wait, at offset 20, if it\'s `0xc0 0x14`, then `*encoded` is `0xc0`, which has `INDIR_MASK` set. So it follows the indirection again. The labels at offset 20 are NOT counted because the code only counts labels when `top == 0x00`. So a chain of indirections doesn\'t count any labels.\n\nBut what if the indirection points to a location with a regular label, and then another indirection points to the same location? Like:\n1. Start at off